    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier confusion; I will stick strictly to the engineering side of your deck calculation.\n\nTo calculate the loads for your Vancouver cedar deck, you first determine the **Dead Load**, which is the permanent weight of the structure itself. This includes the weight of the cedar decking, joists, beams, and posts. In Vancouver, you must also account for the weight of any permanent fixtures like railings or built-in planters.\n\nNext, you calculate the **Live Load**, which represents the temporary weight of people, furniture, and snow. For residential decks in Canada, the standard live load is typically 400 Pa (approx. 80 lbs/sq ft), but you should check the specific National Building Code of Canada (NBC) for British Columbia, as snow loads in Vancouver can be significant.\n\nTo find the **Total Load** for your beam and post sizing, you simply add the Dead Load and the Live Load together. The beam must be sized to support this combined weight without exceeding the allowable stress limits for the wood species you are using. You will use span tables or structural engineering software to determine the minimum post size and beam depth required to safely support this total load over your specific span distance.",
