      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The National Building Code of Canada (NBC) specifies a snow load of 2.4 kPa for Vancouver, but this number can easily be exceeded by a sudden, localized accumulation or a \"snow slide\" event. To prevent unexpected failure, you need to focus on three critical weak points: the **post-to-beam connection**, the **beam-to-post connection**, and the **footing embedment**.\n\n**1. Post-to-Beam Connection (The \"Giant's Neck\" Weak Point)**\nThe most common failure point is where the vertical post meets the horizontal beam. If you simply nail the post to the top of the beam, the shear forces from the snow load can cause the post to shear off or the beam to split.\n*   **Warning Sign:** If the connection relies on nails alone without a metal connector, the wood fibers can tear apart under lateral pressure.\n*   **Fix:** Use a **metal post base** (like a Simpson Strong-Tie) that wraps around the post and bolts into the beam. This distributes the load across the entire width of the beam, preventing the \"neck\" from snapping.\n\n**2. Beam-to-Post Connection (The \"Slippery Slope\")",
